# Line-ending policy for the Samus tree.
#
# core.autocrlf=true at the system level (Git-for-Windows install) checks text
# files out as CRLF on Windows. Two things break when that happens:
#
# 1. Shell scripts in a workcell image get a CRLF shebang, and the Linux
#    container exits 127 on start:
#      /usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
#
# 2. The immutable-baseline drift gate at boot (backend/identity/boot.py)
#    hashes raw file bytes and compares them against the hashes recorded in
#    backend/identity/immutable_baseline.json, which are seeded from LF bytes.
#    A fresh `git worktree add` inherits system core.autocrlf=true and produces
#    CRLF Python/JSON files, so all 12 manifest paths flip to <drift> and the
#    gate refuses to boot.
#
# The main Samus checkout works around this with a local core.autocrlf=false
# in .git/config, but that setting is not inherited by worktrees. Pinning the
# file types below to LF here makes every checkout (main tree, per-agent
# worktree, fresh clone) byte-identical, so `docker compose build` and the
# baseline gate never depend on which checkout produced the bytes.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Git hooks are shebang scripts — CRLF would break the interpreter line.
scripts/hooks/* text eol=lf

# Python and Markdown stay LF on every OS. Windows editors (and some agent
# tooling) otherwise write CRLF, which lands as whole-file EOL diffs and CRLF
# churn in the docs/codex drafts. Pinning eol=lf also keeps the immutable-
# baseline .py byte-stable across checkouts regardless of core.autocrlf, so the
# signature-drift gate cannot flip on a CRLF-converted worktree.
*.py text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
